## 1. The bounded subagent model

Swarm agents do exactly ONE assignment via the runtime's subagent dispatcher
(`runSubagent` on VS Code, `Agent` on Claude Code, `spawn_agent` on Codex) and
then exit. They never call `holly chat wait`, never join a channel, never post
BEGIN/SIGN-OFF, and never wait for peers. The Orchestrator drives the whole
cycle from a single window: dispatch -> collect report -> review -> integrate
-> close.

This is the only multi-agent delivery model. There are no persistent
chat-delivery workers: a swarm agent MUST NEVER be opened as a persistent chat
window or instructed to use `holly chat wait`.

Use swarm delivery when an epic decomposes into granular children with low file
overlap that can each be described completely up front. If the work needs
persistent negotiation, evolving shared context, or continuous reassignment,
stop and use a lighter path: finer decomposition, sequential `/implement`, or
direct handling by the Orchestrator.

### Codex runtime mapping

In Codex, bounded swarm dispatch maps to the `multi_agent_v1.spawn_agent` tool:

- Use `agent_type: "worker"` for implementation and reviewer swarm assignments.
- Set `model` and reasoning effort from `model-capability-tiers.yaml` ->
  `runtimes.codex.projection` keyed on the agent's `capability_tier`. Projection
  is tier-keyed, so reviewers resolve to `gpt-5.5` even at standard strength
  (`standard_review` -> `gpt-5.5`/`high`, `senior_review` -> `gpt-5.5`/`xhigh`),
  NOT `gpt-5.4`. `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` stays an explicit cheaper override for the
  lightest tiny work.
- Codex does not automatically load Holly `.agent.md` files into spawned agents.
  The Orchestrator prompt must include the swarm role envelope, issue ID,
  allowed files or file areas, required skill floor, verification commands, and
  the hard rules: do not close issues, do not merge, do not revert unrelated
  changes, and return a structured report.
- `spawn_agent` returns the requested agent ID/nickname, not an independently
  verified `actual_model`. Record the requested model and model label used for
  audit. Do not treat a worker's self-report as proof of the actual model.

