## 4. Granularity limits and the granularity gate

Each tier has a hard envelope (file/line caps from section 2). The granularity
gate runs before any subagent launch planning and classifies every open child
to the cheapest safe implementation agent:

- **tiny:** one mechanical change, 1-2 files, no cross-module contracts, no
  user-facing judgement, one focused verification command.
- **standard:** one focused fix or implementation slice, 1-4 files, clear
  acceptance criteria, no architecture/migration/auth/security/broad refactor.
- **senior:** one coherent architecture-bearing change with explicit scope —
  cross-cutting refactors, migrations, concurrency, or security-sensitive work
  the cheaper tiers must reject. Up to ~8 files / 600 lines, still one issue.
  Requires a senior model; escalate if none is available.
- **ui-browser:** one scoped UI/browser-flow slice with a known route or
  component, expected states, and browser-verifiable acceptance criteria.

A child is **oversized** if it contains multiple independent behaviours, spans
unrelated subsystems, exceeds the selected agent envelope, requires multiple
unrelated suites or manual judgement, has unclear dependencies, or cannot be
verified from a bounded final report.

For every oversized child, propose a split into smaller replacement children
under the same epic, present the plan to the user, and wait for explicit
approval before changing the tracker. If the user declines splitting, do not
dispatch the oversized child to a cheap agent — route it to direct handling, a
stronger one-shot agent, sequential `/implement`, or defer it.

