## 5. Session bootstrap expectations

How the worker obtains `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` depends on the runtime's dispatch tool:

- **VS Code (`runSubagent`)** — the worker inherits `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` from the
  Orchestrator automatically. It does NOT run `holly session get`, does NOT
  create a new session, and does NOT call `holly session subagent register`
  itself. If the inherited value is missing, the worker STOPs before doing
  task work.
- **Claude Code (`Agent`)** — `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` is NOT auto-wired into the worker's
  shell. The Orchestrator must pre-register each worker with
  `holly session subagent register <ISSUE_ID>` BEFORE dispatch and inject the literal
  `HOLLY_SESSION_ID=sess-...` value into the dispatched prompt. If the Orchestrator
  does not wire the session, the worker STOPs and reports the bootstrap failure.

When the worker has `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID`, the contract every dispatched prompt must
include:

1. **Load the mandatory skills first.** Before any substantive work the worker
   loads its role-based skill floor: the Tier-1 common skills
   (`ax-commandments`, `agent-fs`, `agent-sessions`, `use-workflow`,
   `second-brain`, `qmd`, and the active tracker skill) plus the skills for its
   capability tier and the surface it touches — see "Per-worker skill floor"
   below. If a mandatory skill cannot be loaded, the worker STOPs and reports
   rather than proceeding on a partial floor. Each swarm agent definition also
   carries this list, but the dispatch prompt MUST restate it so a worker on a
   runtime that does not auto-load the agent's skills still gets the floor.
2. **Consult and update project memory.** On task start, load the project
   memory through Holly (per the `project-knowledge` skill): run
   `holly memory read` and `holly memory search "<issue entities>"`. On finish,
   implementation workers record durable, non-obvious facts
   they learned or corrected as `project-knowledge` nodes; reviewers (read-only)
   surface such facts in their report for the Orchestrator to record instead.
   Skip if the project has no memory tree (`holly doctor` warns when it is absent).
3. `HOLLY_SESSION_ID="$HOLLY_SESSION_ID" holly task status`
4. `HOLLY_SESSION_ID="$HOLLY_SESSION_ID" holly session subagent active --json`
5. Find the active subagent entry for the assigned issue ID. Ignore unrelated
   active subagents. If the assigned issue ID is missing, STOP and report the
   bootstrap failure.
6. On completion run exactly:
   `HOLLY_SESSION_ID="$HOLLY_SESSION_ID" holly session subagent complete <ISSUE_ID> --status <status>`.

Reviewers use the same inherited-session bootstrap and also complete their own
active subagent entry with a targeted `holly session subagent complete <ISSUE_ID>`.

### Parent/child session isolation (the task-state contract)

A worker shares the Orchestrator's `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` only for read-only context
and subagent bookkeeping. It MUST NOT run `holly task start` or `holly task finish`
under that inherited id — doing so overwrites the Orchestrator's own
`current_task`/`worktree_path`, and when the worker's worktree is later removed the
Orchestrator is left pointing at a path that no longer exists (the TIZA-873
regression).

Holly enforces this on two levels:

- **Identity:** Claude Code and VS Code use `SubagentStart` to mint a deterministic
  CHILD session id, pre-create a parent-linked child session, and print
  `export HOLLY_SESSION_ID=<child-id>` in startup context. Codex does not emit
  `SubagentStart`; its worker inherits the parent Holly id but receives a unique,
  runtime-owned `CODEX_THREAD_ID`. Every Holly session entry point resolves that
  mismatch to a deterministic parent-linked child before every Holly mutation.
  `holly task start` also emits the child export for its current eval shell. A worker that
  needs its own worktree lifecycle therefore runs under the child id on every
  supported runtime, never the parent id.
- **Guard:** `holly task start` refuses to run on a session that is currently
  parenting running subagents (the Orchestrator mid-dispatch) when the new task
  differs, with a message pointing at the child-id contract. A worker that leaked
  the parent id is stopped before it can corrupt parent state. `--force` is the
  Orchestrator's own escape hatch.

Subagent bookkeeping (`holly session subagent active`/`complete`) always resolves
to the parent's records, whether the worker prefixes the parent id or its child
id — so the parent can always inspect and complete its children. Use
`holly session subagent list --json` to inspect active and completed subagents,
including each worker's `child_session_id`.

For Codex, fresh tool calls also start fresh shells, so shell exports are not the
cross-command guarantee. Runtime-thread re-resolution is. `holly task status
--json` is the authoritative bootstrap check: its `session_id` must differ from
`inherited_session_id` before the worker manages a worktree. If it does not, stop
rather than passing `--force`.

In the normal swarm flow workers do not run the task lifecycle at all (see §7,
integration ownership): they push a branch and stop, and the Orchestrator
integrates. The child-session mechanism exists for the adapter flows (e.g. a
claim/release flow) where a worker genuinely owns its worktree end to end — the
concrete commands live in the active adapter addendum, not here.

### Per-worker skill floor

The role-based skill matrix in the `holly` skill is the source of truth; this is
the per-tier summary the Orchestrator restates in each dispatch prompt. If a
required skill is missing in the consumer project, install it with
`holly skills install <name>` or escalate — never dispatch onto a partial floor.

- **Every worker (Tier 1):** `ax-commandments`, `agent-fs`, `agent-sessions`,
  `use-workflow`, `second-brain`, `qmd`, the active tracker skill (resolved per
  adapter — see the `issue-tracker` addendum), and `issue-tracker` itself for the
  read-only verbs the worker is allowed.
- **Implementation** (`swarm-tiny`/`swarm-standard`/`swarm-senior-implementation`):
  also `test-driven-development` and `systematic-debugging`; `adr-skill` for
  senior; plus the Frontend or Backend specialization skills for whatever the
  assigned diff touches.
- **`swarm-ui-browser-implementation`:** also the Frontend specialization,
  `ui-visual-capture` (required — capture every viewport before and after), and
  `webapp-testing`.
- **Reviewers** (`swarm-reviewer`/`swarm-senior-reviewer`): also the
  `review-implementation` workflow as the review-standards source of truth; add
  `ui-visual-capture` and `webapp-testing` when the change under review touches UI.

