## 9. Adapter differences

The `orchestrate-swarm` workflow is adapter-specific and selected by sync from
the active adapter package; only its tracker commands differ, not the swarm
model. Read the active adapter addendum in the `issue-tracker` skill for the
concrete command surface. The shape of the differences:

| Capability | Notes |
|------------|-------|
| **Full-featured adapter** | Ships a complete `orchestrate-swarm` workflow with automated dependency/DAG analysis, structured decomposition gates, and explicit tracker state sync in postflight. |
| **Reduced adapter** | Ships a workflow with a shorter preflight when the tracker has no DAG-validation tooling; wave ordering is derived manually from parent/child relationships and descriptions. Closure may use a comment-then-handoff sequence rather than a single close call. May still be pending — see its addendum. |
| **Unknown adapter** | Receives a hard-blocking stub that refuses to run rather than guessing tracker semantics. |

When the active adapter provides no swarm workflow, stop and escalate rather
than improvise tracker commands. Adapter-specific command detail — canonical
mention format, release/claim semantics, and worktree commands — belongs in the
adapter addendum and the active adapter's `orchestrate-swarm` workflow, never in
this skill. Where a worker owns its worktree through a claim/release flow, that
lifecycle runs under the worker's CHILD session id (§5, parent/child isolation)
so it never rewrites the Orchestrator's task state.

